---
/**
 * hospitality / TestimonialsBK — "Villa Marindu" IN THEIR WORDS: a centred head
 * (brass eyebrow over a Prata headline) above a three-up row of night-panel
 * quote cards, each opened by a rating mark, then the quote at Prata 19.5px and
 * a tracked-caps attribution.
 *
 * ROLE: the social proof a whole-villa booking is made on — which is exactly
 * why this file prints nothing the owner did not put on record.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value here is lifted from the
 * TESTIMONIALS section of "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html":
 *   head            text-align centre, 48px below it
 *   eyebrow         10px / .44em / caps / brass / 600      → .kick, unchanged
 *   headline        Prata 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.1, margin .26em 0 0
 *   grid            repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)), gap 26px
 *   card            #141A18 ground, 1px #262E2B, radius 3px, padding 32px 30px
 *   mark            13px, brass, letter-spacing 3px, 18px below it
 *   quote           Prata 19.5px / 1.55, #F4F1EA, 20px below it
 *   attribution     11px / .2em / caps / #767C76
 * Mapped to wrapper tokens with the same value: #141A18 = --night-2,
 * #262E2B = --rule, 3px = --r-lg, #B99552 = --accent, #F4F1EA = --bone-hi,
 * #767C76 = --muted. No literal hex appears below.
 *
 * READS content.testimonials.items ({quote, who, rating}); tolerates the legacy
 * bare array at content.testimonials and the legacy author/name spellings for
 * the attribution. The data-field always names the CANONICAL path, so the first
 * sidebar edit migrates a legacy row onto the real key.
 *
 * COERCED AT EVERY READ. Nothing upstream type-checks a row at runtime, and this
 * family has already shipped one "x.slice is not a function" page: a quote that
 * arrives as a number crashes the build on .trim(), and one that arrives as an
 * object prints "[object Object]" through set:html. str() passes strings and
 * finite numbers and treats everything else as absent. Blank-after-trim counts
 * as absent too — a whitespace-only `who` must not draw an empty figcaption and
 * a whitespace-only tag must not draw an empty brass eyebrow.
 * astro-builder's normalizeBlock aliases name/author onto `who` only when `who`
 * is null, so a row carrying who:'' keeps its blank; the fallback chain below is
 * `||` and not `??` so that row still prints the name the owner did type.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one is declared in genericContentSchema, group
 * "testimonials" — a data-field on a path the schema does not own is a dead
 * hook that no sidebar input can focus)
 *   testimonials.tag                  — the brass eyebrow
 *   testimonials.headline             — set:html, so an owner can place the
 *                                       template's italic-brass emphasis
 *                                       anywhere inside their own sentence
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.quote     — set:html, the Prata blockquote
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.who       — the tracked-caps figcaption
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.rating    — read, never printed as text: it is the
 *                                       ONLY thing that can draw a star (below).
 *                                       It deliberately carries NO data-field on
 *                                       the star row, and that is not a missing
 *                                       hook — the row RENDERS the number, it is
 *                                       not the number. editorBridge's ed:update
 *                                       would replace the glyphs with the raw
 *                                       digit, and VisualEditor (which does not
 *                                       honour data-derived) makes every
 *                                       [data-field] contentEditable, so an
 *                                       admin could type "★★★★★" back into
 *                                       rating and lose the score. It is edited
 *                                       in the sidebar's "Rating out of 5" box,
 *                                       which genericContentSchema declares.
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored: the number of stars, and the reveal delay (capped at
 * 3 so a fourth card is not still hidden a second after it scrolls in).
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION — the hard rule of this section:
 *   - THE STAR ROW IS NOT HARDCODED. The design prints "★★★★★" over every quote.
 *     Five stars for a guest who never gave a score is a claim invented on the
 *     owner's behalf, so stars are drawn ONLY from a finite numeric
 *     testimonials.items.${i}.rating. Non-numeric, blank and absent ratings draw
 *     no stars at all; the card instead opens with the design's brass hairline,
 *     which occupies the same beat and states nothing. Expect the hairline on
 *     essentially every real site — that is the correct, honest default, and it
 *     is not a missing feature.
 *   - CLAMPING, stated so it is not read as an oversight: a rating is rounded,
 *     then capped at 5 (a typo of "7" draws five stars, never seven). A value
 *     that rounds BELOW 1 — 0, -2, 0.4 — is treated as no usable rating and
 *     draws the hairline. It is never clamped UP to one star, because inventing
 *     a star out of a zero is the same fabrication as inventing five.
 *   - The accessible name announces the SAME number that is drawn, so the label
 *     and the glyphs can never disagree.
 *   - NO review count, NO average score, NO platform name, NO "guest favourite"
 *     or "superhost" badge. None of it is in the data contract.
 *   - Quote and attribution are guarded independently, and every guard sits
 *     OUTSIDE its tag, so a quote with no name renders as an unattributed quote
 *     rather than an empty caption with a bottom margin.
 *   - A row with no quote is DROPPED: the card IS the quote, so a name-only row
 *     would render a bordered box attributing nothing to somebody. The row stays
 *     in the sidebar and reappears the instant a quote is typed.
 *
 * SECTION GATE: the whole section renders only when at least one row has a real
 * quote. It deliberately does NOT count tag/headline: a head arrives from the
 * admin sidebar or the AI extractor with nothing behind it all the time, and
 * unlike why/how/faq this block is not even handed a derived default by
 * astro-builder — so a gate that counted a headline would ship a centred heading
 * over an empty grid, which is the one thing a proof section must never do.
 *
 * ORIGINAL INDEXES: `i` is captured before blank rows are dropped, so removing
 * row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 4. Nothing here is numbered
 * on screen, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * THE ONE AUTHORED STRING in this file is the stars' aria-label, which is never
 * visible. It exists because a row of glyphs with no accessible name is
 * decoration announcing nothing, and a rating an owner typed is information a
 * screen-reader user is entitled to.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   testimonials.source   — the schema declares it, but this template has an
 *                           honest home for a star claim (rating), and the
 *                           design draws no source line under the head.
 *   testimonials.bigQuote — no pull-quote element in this design (family
 *                           precedent: BI and BJ do not render it either).
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.role — the row schema stops at quote/who/rating, so
 *                           the hook would focus nothing in the v3 sidebar.
 *
 * NO ANCHOR HELPER, and that is not an omission: this section emits no <a> at
 * all (the design's testimonials block contains no link), so there is no href to
 * remap and no data-href-field to own. It also takes NO id — the page's anchor
 * set is about / services / gallery / location / faq / main, and the design
 * gives this section none of them.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME NOTE (engine): this section must NOT be class "testi" —
 * buildOverrideCss pins `.testi, .testi p { color: <on-ink> !important }`, which
 * would repaint the quotes with a scheme colour the design never chose. It is
 * class "voices" and carries no buttons at all, so no engine label pin reaches
 * it.
 *
 * CONTRAST: the attribution is --muted on --night-2 = 4.13:1, below AA. That is
 * the design's own pairing and the wrapper's frontmatter records it as accepted
 * rather than silently altered; the line carries only a name, and lifting
 * --muted is the owner's one-line call in the wrapper, not this file's.
 *
 * VERTICAL RHYTHM comes from the wrapper's shared `section` padding — the
 * design's "100px top, 0 bottom" is the same rhythm once every section stacks.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const test = content.testimonials || {};

/* Coerce at EVERY read — see COERCED AT EVERY READ above. A string passes; a
   finite number passes as its digits; null, undefined, NaN, an array and an
   object are not content and become ''. Nothing downstream calls a string method
   on raw input, so no shape from the pipeline can fail the build. */
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : (typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '');

const tag = str(test.tag).trim();
const headline = str(test.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  quote?: unknown;
  who?: unknown;
  author?: unknown;
  name?: unknown;
  rating?: unknown;
};

/* Tolerate both shapes at every read: the canonical object with .items, and the
   legacy bare array parked at content.testimonials. */
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(test.items)
  ? test.items
  : (Array.isArray(test) ? (test as Item[]) : []);

/* A rating is a CLAIM about a guest. Only a finite number becomes stars; it is
   rounded, capped at 5, and anything that rounds below 1 yields 0 — which draws
   the hairline, not a star. Never widen this. */
const starsOf = (it: Item): number => {
  const r = Number(it.rating);
  if (!Number.isFinite(r)) return 0;
  const n = Math.round(r);
  return n >= 1 ? Math.min(5, n) : 0;
};

const STAR = '★';

/* Normalise ONCE, carrying the ORIGINAL array index, then drop rows that cannot
   render an honest card. Gating on `rows` rather than `items` also means a block
   of blank rows hides instead of shipping three empty bordered boxes. */
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({
    /* the quote keeps its own leading/trailing space — only the GUARD is
       trimmed, so a blank-looking quote cannot open a bordered card */
    quote: str(it.quote),
    who: str(it.who).trim() || str(it.author).trim() || str(it.name).trim(),
    stars: starsOf(it),
    i,
  }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.quote.trim()));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="voices">
    <div class="wrap">
      {hasHead && (
        <div class="v-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="testimonials.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2" data-field="testimonials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}
      <div class="v-grid">
        {rows.map((row, k) => (
          <figure class="v-card reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            {row.stars > 0 ? (
              /* Drawn from the owner's own number, and named with that same
                 number so the glyphs are never decoration-only. */
              <span class="v-stars" role="img" aria-label={`Rated ${row.stars} out of 5`}>
                <span aria-hidden="true">{STAR.repeat(row.stars)}</span>
              </span>
            ) : (
              /* Where the design hardcodes five stars. A brass hairline on the
                 same beat: same visual opening, no claim. */
              <span class="v-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            )}
            <blockquote data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.quote`} set:html={row.quote}></blockquote>
            {row.who && (
              <figcaption data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.who`}>{row.who}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── head · centred, 48px of air under it (design) ───────────────────────── */
  .v-head { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 48px; }
  .v-head .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* The wrapper's .h2 is left-aligned with a bottom margin; the design's head is
     centred with the margin on TOP. The 24ch cap from the wrapper is kept (and
     centred by the auto margins) so a long owner headline cannot run the full
     1280px of the container. */
  .voices .h2 {
    margin: 0.26em auto 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── grid · three up, 26px (design) ──────────────────────────────────────── */
  .v-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 26px;
  }

  /* ── card · the design's panel tone, hairline and SHARP 3px corner ───────── */
  .v-card {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    background-color: var(--night-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    padding: 32px 30px;
  }

  /* ── the rating mark. Both variants occupy ~40px so a card with a rating and
        a card without stay on the same baseline in the same row. ──────────── */
  .v-stars {
    display: block;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  /* the hairline that replaces the design's hardcoded star row — 72px is the
     width those five glyphs would have occupied */
  .v-rule {
    display: block;
    width: 72px;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    background-color: var(--accent);
    margin: 11px 0 28px;
  }

  /* ── quote · Prata at the design's 19.5px. The UA gives <blockquote> a 40px
        side margin, so the reset here is load-bearing, not tidying. ────────── */
  .v-card blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 19.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bone-hi);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* set:html output carries no scope class, so an owner's <em> is only reachable
     through :global(). It stays the quote's own colour: the italic-brass
     emphasis is the HEADLINE signature, and repainting a guest's words brass
     would read as the villa talking, not the guest. */
  .v-card blockquote :global(em),
  .v-card blockquote :global(.em) { font-style: italic; color: inherit; }

  /* ── attribution · the design's 11px / .2em, not .mono's 10px / .22em / 500 */
  .v-card figcaption {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE · this section owns its own grid; the wrapper owns the shared
        760 / 520 type pass. ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .v-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 22px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .v-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .v-head { margin-bottom: 34px; }
    .voices .h2 { max-width: none; }
    .v-card { padding: 24px 20px; }
    .v-card blockquote { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.5; margin-bottom: 16px; }
    .v-card figcaption { letter-spacing: 0.16em; }
    .v-stars { letter-spacing: 2px; }
  }
</style>
